{
  "requirement": "HC MUST use one {term}`requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.validator` per assessment. Its usage record MUST report that validator’s attempt and token totals under {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.Validator.usage` and its failure and retry counts under {cite}`requirements.authoring.usage_requests.failures`. The record MUST distinguish partial or unavailable token measurements from complete totals and preserve the available cache and reasoning breakdowns. The assessment MUST also report elapsed time.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.authoring.usage_requests`.\n\n**One validator per assessment, and its usage in the record.**\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#fn[run]` builds one `Validator` inside `AssessmentUsage`. The `drop` impl copies `validator.usage()` into the log's `provider_usage`. I read both to see how the validator's usage reaches the record.\n- `throttled_requests_retry_unchanged_and_record_every_attempt` in `src/authoring/mod.rs` runs a full `assess` call with 5 throttled failures and a final success. It asserts these `provider_usage` fields:\n  - `attempts == 6`, `retries == 5` and `failed_attempts == 5`.\n  - Per-category token totals as `{reported, missing_attempts}` for `input_tokens`, `output_tokens`, `cached_input_tokens`, `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `reasoning_tokens`.\n  - `prepared_inputs` and `delivered_inputs`.\n- The structural \"one validator\" wording has no dedicated test. A second validator would not give these totals, so I treat the totals as adequate evidence.\n\n**Failure and retry counts.**\n- The same throttled test checks the per-request failure records (`http_status`, `error_code`, `retried: true`) and that the final request has no `failure`.\n- `failed_submission_can_be_confirmed_on_a_later_attempt` in `src/authoring/usage.rs` checks `retried` flipping from false to true.\n- The requirement cites `usage_requests.failures` for these counts. That requirement has its own tests, and I did not reassess them here.\n\n**Partial or unavailable token measurements versus complete totals, and cache and reasoning breakdowns.**\n- `token_totals_keep_subsets_separate_and_missing_counters_unknown` covers these cases:\n  - An in-flight request gives `complete == false`.\n  - Two measured requests give `complete == true` with summed input, output, cached, cache-creation and reasoning counts.\n  - A failed request with no usage makes `complete` false while the earlier totals are kept.\n- `partial_failure_retains_available_totals_and_optional_measurements` checks that optional counters stay null when never reported, and that a reported `cached_input` of 0 is kept.\n- The throttled test checks the missing-attempt counts for partial totals.\n- `claude_requests_carry_cache_checkpoints_and_record_cache_usage` asserts per-request and total cache read and write tokens.\n- `openai_responses_replays_tool_calls_and_records_usage` asserts cached and reasoning totals.\n- The Ollama and Bedrock CLI tests assert input and output totals and `complete`.\n- `cancelled_request_persists_elapsed_time_and_incomplete_totals` covers incomplete totals after cancellation.\n\n**Elapsed time.**\n- `completed_requests_and_the_run_record_elapsed_time` asserts per-request `elapsed_ms == 20` and run `elapsed_ms >= 50`. It also asserts that the run elapsed time is at least the request elapsed time and that the request value is unchanged after `finish`.\n- `cancelled_request_persists_elapsed_time_and_incomplete_totals` asserts elapsed time on a cancelled request.\n- Elapsed time is not asserted on a full CLI run. The behaviour is unconditional and is covered at the log level, so I do not count this as a gap.\n\n**Terminal failure.** Totals survive a terminal failure in `token_totals_keep_subsets_separate_and_missing_counters_unknown`, which ends with `finish(\"failed\")` and still reports the earlier totals. The failure-count side is delegated to `usage_requests.failures`.\n\nI found no unacknowledged gaps, and there are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments to account for.",
  "references": "690ccd5d774a163bfa61c7627bb1240393187d61c644988217fdf8ac050a5d06",
  "inputs": [
